      "generated_answer": "Leopold, particularly in *A Sand County Almanac*, emphasizes that land use involves ethical responsibilities beyond mere economics—his “land ethic” expands morality to include soils, waters, plants, and animals. While he critiques purely economic valuations (e.g., rejecting the idea that nature’s worth is only monetary), he does not dismiss all economic considerations outright, nor does he reduce environmental issues solely to human interpersonal ethics. He also explicitly rejects the simplistic notion of “balance of nature,” instead favoring ecological complexity and interdependence.\n\n\\boxed{C}",
