    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Use indirect truth tables to determine whether the following argument is valid. If the argument is invalid, choose an option which presents a counterexample. (There may be other counterexamples as well.)\n(K · L) ⊃ M\nN ⊃ ~M\nN ∨ K / L ⊃ K\n\nOptions: \nA. Valid\nB. Invalid. Counterexample when L, M, and N are true and K is false\nC. Invalid. Counterexample when L and M are true and K and N are false\nD. Invalid. Counterexample when L and N are true and K and M are false\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "Qwen/Qwen3-Coder-Next",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "We test the argument using indirect truth tables: assume premises true and conclusion false to find a counterexample.  \nConclusion $L \\supset K$ is false only when $L = T$ and $K = F$.  \nPremise 1: $(K \\cdot L) \\supset M$ becomes $(F \\cdot T) \\supset M = F \\supset M$, which is true for any $M$.  \nPremise 2: $N \\supset \\sim M$ must be true. Premise 3: $N \\lor K = N \\lor F = N$, so $N$ must be true.  \nThen $N = T$ and premise 2 gives $\\sim M = T$, so $M = F$.  \nThus, $L = T, K = F, N = T, M = F$ makes all premises true and conclusion false — a valid counterexample.  \nOption D matches this assignment.\n\n\\boxed{D}",
