    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Which one of the following statements is true concerning VARs?\n\nOptions: \nA. The coefficient estimates in VARs are always positive\nB. VARs require all variables to be of the same sign for effective forecasting\nC. The coefficient estimates have intuitive theoretical interpretations\nD. VARs often produce better forecasts than simultaneous equation structural models\nE. All of the components of a VAR must be stationary before it can be used for forecasting\nF. The coefficient estimates have no theoretical interpretations in VARs\nG. VARs cannot be used for forecasting purposes\nH. All components of a VAR must be non-stationary for accurate forecasting\nI. VARs produce poorer forecasts when compared to standalone variable models\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "grok-4-1-fast-reasoning",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "**D**\n\nVARs are reduced-form models that often outperform simultaneous equation structural models in out-of-sample forecasting due to fewer restrictive assumptions. Coefficient estimates in VARs lack intuitive structural interpretations (making C and F incorrect), and standard VARs require stationary variables (contra E and H). Other options misstate VAR properties.\n\n\\boxed{D}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning process evaluates each option against established econometric properties of Vector Autoregressions (VARs). Options A, B, G, H, and I are dismissed as they contradict core VAR characteristics: coefficients are not always positive (A), no sign requirement exists (B), VARs are used for forecasting (G), stationarity—not non-stationarity—is typically needed (H), and VARs generally forecast better than univariate models (I). Option E is incorrect because, while stationarity is required for standard VARs, cointegrated systems use VECM, a VAR variant. Options C and F are evaluated next: VAR coefficients lack intuitive structural interpretations (ruling out C), but F is not selected as the primary truth. Option D is identified as correct, as econometric literature (e.g., Sims' critique) highlights VARs' superior out-of-sample forecasting performance over simultaneous equation structural models due to reduced misspecification risks. Thus, D aligns most accurately with empirical evidence and theory.",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 196,
        "output_tokens": 269,
