      "generated_answer": "**C**\n\nA budget deficit occurs when government spending exceeds revenues in a given year, increasing the federal debt; conversely, a negative deficit (surplus) reduces the debt. Option C correctly states this relationship. Other options confuse or reverse the definitions.\n\n\\boxed{C}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning process starts by defining key terms: the federal budget deficit as annual spending exceeding revenues, which adds to the debt, while a surplus (negative deficit) reduces it. Options are then evaluated systematically: A is incorrect because debt reflects past deficits minus surpluses, not future ones; B, E, I, and J fail due to debt typically being positive with no direct causal link to deficit changes; D and F reverse the relationship; G is false as they differ in scale and timing; H contradicts the deficit's effect. This elimination confirms C as correct, as a negative deficit directly decreases the debt.",
