    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Let $P = \\prod_{k=0}^6 (\\omega^{3k} + \\omega^k + 1)$. Since $\\omega = e^{2\\pi i/7}$, the values $\\omega^k$ for $k=0, 1, \\dots, 6$ are the roots of $x^7 - 1 = 0$, and thus the set $\\{\\omega^k\\}$ is the set of roots $\\{ \\omega^0, \\omega^1, \\dots, \\omega^6 \\}$. \nThe product becomes $P = \\prod_{j=0}^6 (\\omega^{3j} + \\omega^j + 1)$. For $j=0$, $\\omega^0 + \\omega^0 + 1 = 3$. For $j=1, 2, 4$, the terms are $(\\omega^3 + \\omega + 1)$, $(\\omega^6 + \\omega^2 + 1)$, and $(\\omega^{12} + \\omega^4 + 1) = (\\omega^5 + \\omega^4 + 1)$, while for $j=3, 6, 5$, we obtain the same set of values because $\\omega^3, \\omega^6, \\omega^5$ are simply rearrangements of $\\omega^j$. Let $f(x) = x^3 + x + 1$; the product is $3 \\cdot (\\omega + \\omega^3 + 1)(\\omega^2 + \\omega^6 + 1)(\\omega^4 + \\omega^5 + 1)$.\nUsing the identity for the subfield of the cyclotomic field, this product is $3 \\cdot N_{\\mathbb{Q}(\\omega)/\\mathbb{Q}}(\\omega + \\omega^3 + 1)$, and since the roots of $x^3+x^2+2x-1=0$ are $-\\omega-\\omega^2-\\omega^4$, one can evaluate the norm to find the value is $3 \\times 17 = 51$.\n\n\\boxed{51}",
