      "generated_answer": "We are given that $ n + 2 $ divides $ 3(n + 3)(n^2 + 9) $, and we are to find the sum of all positive integers $ n $ satisfying this condition.\n\nLet $ d = n + 2 $, so $ n = d - 2 $. Substituting into the expression:\n$$\n3(n + 3)(n^2 + 9) = 3((d - 2) + 3)((d - 2)^2 + 9) = 3(d + 1)(d^2 - 4d + 4 + 9) = 3(d + 1)(d^2 - 4d + 13)\n$$\nWe need $ d \\mid 3(d + 1)(d^2 - 4d + 13) $. Since $ d $ divides this expression and $ \\gcd(d, d+1) = 1 $, $ d $ must divide $ 3(d^2 - 4d + 13) $. But $ d \\mid d^2 $ and $ d \\mid 4d $, so $ d \\mid 3(d^2 - 4d + 13) $ implies $ d \\mid 3 \\cdot 13 = 39 $. Thus, $ d $ is a positive divisor of 39, so possible $ d = 1, 3, 13, 39 $ (positive since $ n > 0 \\Rightarrow d = n+2 \\geq 3 $, so discard $ d=1 $).\n\nThus valid $ d = 3, 13, 39 $, so $ n = d - 2 = 1, 11, 37 $. Check each:\n- $ n = 1 $: $ n+2 = 3 $, expression: $ 3(4)(1 + 9) = 3 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 10 = 120 $, $ 3 \\mid 120 $: yes.\n- $ n = 11 $: $ n+2 = 13 $, expression: $ 3(14)(121 + 9) = 3 \\cdot 14 \\cdot 130 = 5460 $, $ 13 \\mid 5460 $? $ 5460 / 13 = 420 $: yes.\n- $ n = 37 $: $ n+2 = 39 $, expression: $ 3(40)(1369 + 9) = 3 \\cdot 40 \\cdot 1378 = 165360 $, $ 39 \\mid 165360 $? $ 165360 / 39 = 4240 $: yes.\n\nAll work. Sum: $ 1 + 11 + 37 = 49 $.\n\n\\boxed{49}",
