    "global index": "MMLUPro_math_7462",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Can we use the method of compass and straightedge construction to construct the edge length of a cube, such that the volume of the cube is equal to X time the volume of a given cube, where X belongs to the set {{3,17,8,27,343,1331}}? Return the answer list for the respective values of X with 1 for yes and 0 for no.\n\nOptions: \nA. [1, 0, 1, 0, 1, 0]\nB. [1, 1, 0, 0, 0, 0]\nC. [0, 0, 1, 1, 1, 1]\nD. [0, 1, 1, 1, 0, 0]\nE. [0, 1, 1, 0, 0, 1]\nF. [0, 1, 0, 1, 0, 1]\nG. [0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0]\nH. [1, 1, 1, 0, 0, 1]\nI. [1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1]\nJ. [1, 0, 0, 1, 1, 0]\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "grok-4-1-fast-reasoning",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "\\boxed{C}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning begins by recognizing that compass and straightedge constructions produce lengths in field extensions of the rationals with degrees that are powers of 2. For a cube with volume X times that of a given unit cube, the required edge length is the cube root of X, so √[3]{X} must lie in such an extension.\n\nNext, the values are analyzed: 3 and 17 are primes, so their cube roots have minimal polynomials t^3 - 3 and t^3 - 17, both irreducible over Q by Eisenstein's criterion, yielding degree-3 extensions. Since 3 does not divide any 2^k, √[3]{3} and √[3]{17} are not constructible.\n\nFor 8=2^3, 27=3^3, 343=7^3, and 1331=11^3, the cube roots are 2, 3, 7, and 11, respectively, which are rational integers and thus constructible. This yields the list [0, 0, 1, 1, 1, 1], matching option C.",
