      "generated_answer": "**C**\n\nThe system is \\(\\dot{X} = AX + bu\\) with \\(u(t) = \\delta(t)\\), so there is an instantaneous jump at \\(t=0\\), giving \\(X(0^+) = X_0 + b = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix}\\).  \nThe solution is then \\(X(t) = e^{At} X(0^+)\\) for \\(t > 0\\), and computation shows the first component \\(X_1(t) = 0\\) for all \\(t \\ge 0^+\\).  \nThe question likely asks for this value of \\(X_1(t)\\), which is \\(0\\).\n\n\\boxed{C}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning begins by recognizing the system as \\(\\dot{X} = A X + b u\\) with \\(u(t) = \\delta(t)\\), where the Dirac delta impulse at \\(t=0\\) causes an instantaneous jump in the state: \\(X(0^+) = X_0 + b = \\begin{pmatrix} -1 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix} + \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix}\\).  \nFor \\(t > 0\\), the solution is \\(X(t) = e^{A t} X(0^+)\\).  \nComputing \\(e^{A t}\\) via diagonalization (eigenvalues 1 and 2, with \\(e^{A t} = \\begin{pmatrix} e^t & 0 \\\\ 2 e^t - 2 e^{2 t} & e^{2 t} \\end{pmatrix}\\)) yields \\(X(t) = e^{A t} \\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\\\ 2 e^{2 t} \\end{pmatrix}\\), so the first component \\(X_1(t) = 0\\) for all \\(t \\geq 0^+\\).  \nGiven the scalar options and the distinctive constant value of \\(X_1(t)\\), option C (0) is the most likely correct answer, as it matches this key feature of the solution.",
