    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "**D**\n\nStandard blood bank protocols require returning a unit out of refrigeration beyond the time limit (typically 30 minutes for RBCs) to the blood bank for evaluation and likely discard, ensuring safety and traceability. Options like reusing, discarding without notification, or improper storage risk hemolysis, bacterial growth, or transfusion reactions. Notifying the blood bank maintains inventory control and documentation.\n\n\\boxed{D}\n\n## Explanation\n\nThe reasoning process starts by identifying the core principle of blood transfusion safety: red blood cells must remain refrigerated at 2-6°C, with a typical out-of-fridge limit of 30 minutes to prevent hemolysis or bacterial contamination. Each option is then systematically evaluated against this standard—options A, B, C, F, H, I, and J are ruled out for proposing unsafe reuse or improper handling that could endanger patients. Options E and G are eliminated due to the need for blood bank notification for traceability and inventory management, leaving D as the correct choice, as it aligns with protocols requiring immediate return and reporting to the blood bank for proper disposal or assessment.",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 219,
