s.\n\nOption D: [C_EG_A] — assuming _E is E (but _E usually means E♭), but in key C, perhaps accidentals are ignored? Or typo.\n\nPerhaps the correct answer is **D**, interpreted as C, E, G, B♭ — C7 chord with E (third) in the bass? But C is listed first.\n\nUnless the bass is not the first note? But in ABC, the chord [C_EG_A] means all notes played, but the bass is likely C.\n\nFor first inversion, the third must be in the bass.\n\nOption **B**: [A,_EFc] — A, E♭, F, C — if A is in the bass, this is not likely a dominant 7th first inversion unless it’s F7 with A in bass — A is the third of F7 (F-A-C-E♭), so if the chord is F, A, C, E♭, but here it's A, E♭, F, C — so A is bass — yes! Third of F7 is A, so first inversion of F7.\n\nSo [A,E♭,F,C] is F7 in first inversion.\n\nThus, **B** is correct.\n\nBut let’s check the notation: “A,_EFc” — A,, E♭, F, c — so notes: A♭? No — A, is A below middle C, _E is E♭, F, c is C.\n\nSo: A, E♭, F, C — so A, E♭, F, C — that's A, E♭, F, C — F major with A and C, but E♭ suggests F7.\n\nNotes: F, A, C, E♭ — F7. In this chord, A is in the bass — A is the third — so first inversion. Yes.\n\nSo **B** is correct.\n\nNow the options are:\n\nA. AB  \nB. BC  \nC. CD  \nD. AD\n\nThe question asks: \"which of the following chords belongs...\" and lists A, B, C, D as chords.\n\nThen the options are pairs.\n\nWe determined that chord **B** is the correct one.\n\nBut the choices are pairs — so is there more than one?\n\nOption A: [F,B,DG] — F, B, D, G — F, B, D is F major, G — could be Fmaj7 add G? Not a major-minor seventh.\n\nMajor-minor seventh = dominant 7th = major triad + minor seventh.\n\nF7 is F-A-C-E♭.\n\nThis has B natural — which is not in F7.\n\nB natural suggests F#? Not likely.\n\nSo not A.\n\nC: [^^F,^A,^C^D] — ^^F is G (if transposed), ^A is A, ^C is C, ^D is D — G, A, C, D — G minor 7? But G-B-D-F is G7, this has A and C — not B.\n\nNot a dominant 7th.\n\nD: [C_EG_A] — C, E, G, A — if _E is E natural, then C, E, G, A — C6, not C7.