    "prediction": "grok-4-1-fast-reasoning",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Jean Dauberval was an 18th-century ballet master noted for elevating character and demi-caractère roles within otherwise noble ballet technique. Historical accounts of his choreography (especially in works such as *La Fille mal gardée*) single out his rehabilitation of the so-called “false” cabrioles—steps in which the working leg opens to the rear rather than the front, literally reversing the direction of the noble or “true” cabriole. These reversed versions had previously been dismissed as suitable only for grotesque portrayals of sailors, drunkards, and comic characters; Dauberval’s acceptance of them helped legitimize a wider expressive range in classical dance.\n\nThus the techniques referenced are cabrioles.\n\n\\boxed{cabrioles (false/reversed)}",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 236,
        "output_tokens": 805,
